[0m[0m
[1mWhat the program does:[22m reads one float via [38;5;10mscanf("%f")[0m and dumps its 4-byte object representation as lowercase hex plus a newline. So all output fidelity reduces to reproducing glibc's float parsing bit-for-bit — [38;5;10mmain.rs[0m mirrors the C structure ([38;5;10mprint_hex[0m/[38;5;10mdriver[0m/[38;5;10mmain[0m), and [38;5;10mcfloat.rs[0m implements the conversion.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mTwo behaviors I had to discover empirically rather than assume.[22m My first version modeled [38;5;10mstrtof[0m, which the C standard describes as what [38;5;10m%f[0m uses. Differential testing showed glibc's [38;5;10mvfscanf[0m disagrees with its own [38;5;10mstrtof[0m in two ways, and I verified each with a direct [38;5;10mstrtof[0m probe before changing anything:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mvfscanf[0m matches the token against [38;5;10m"infinity"[0m and accepts it only at length exactly 3 or 8. [38;5;10m"infi"[0m and [38;5;10m"infinit"[0m are conversion [3mfailures[23m, leaving [38;5;10mx[0m at its initial [38;5;10m0.0[0m → [38;5;10m00000000[0m. Bare [38;5;10mstrtof("infi")[0m returns infinity.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mvfscanf[0m never collects the [38;5;10m(n-char-sequence)[0m after [38;5;10mnan[0m, so payloads are dropped: [38;5;10mscanf[0m on [38;5;10m"nan(123)"[0m gives plain [38;5;10m0000c07f[0m, while [38;5;10mstrtof[0m gives [38;5;10m7b00c07f[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Per the "don't fix bugs" requirement I reproduced the quirks as-is, including the failure path that silently leaves [38;5;10mx[0m at zero. Decimal parsing delegates to Rust's correctly-rounded [38;5;10mparse::<f32>()[0m (agrees with glibc); hex floats use a hand-written round-half-to-even path with a sticky bit, which I stress-tested at the 24-bit tie boundaries, the subnormal/normal transition, and significands exceeding 64 bits.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mOne caveat worth flagging:[22m I initially staged the reference binary in [38;5;10m/tmp/cref[0m, and mid-session another process on this machine overwrote it (that directory contains files I never created, and an exec of my binary failed with [38;5;10mPermissionError[0m). I treated all results up to that point as void, rebuilt the reference in a private [38;5;10mmktemp -d[0m directory, and re-ran everything from scratch — the 23,093-case figures come from that clean run, with md5sums checked before and after to confirm neither binary was swapped mid-run, and a from-scratch [38;5;10mrm -rf target[0m rebuild reproducing the exact binary that was verified.[0m[0m
